        "caption": "📌 SAVE THIS — How to Retry a Failed Test (TestNG)\n\n⚠️ Freeze on this and the interviewer knows you’ve never dealt with a flaky suite 👇\n\n✅ THE MECHANISM:\n✅ In TestNG you don’t wrap tests in loops — you use a built-in hook, IRetryAnalyzer.\n✅ Implement its retry() method, set a max retry count, and it tells TestNG to re-run the failed test up to N times before marking it truly failed.\n\n⚠️ THE SENIOR LINE (this is what separates you):\n✅ Retries are a crutch, not a cure. Blindly retrying HIDES flakiness — a test that only passes on the third attempt is still broken; you’re masking a real timing or sync bug.\n✅ Retry to survive a genuine network blip, but TRACK every retry and fix the root cause. A retry that silently turns red into green is how a broken suite looks healthy.\n\nThe rule:\nTestNG uses IRetryAnalyzer with a max count → but track your retries and fix the flakiness, don’t let retries hide it.\n\n💬 If this helped, grab my Ultimate Interview Success Kit for SDETs — 14 files in one:\n✅ 300+ Interview Q&A with answer strategy\n✅ 100+ Coding Questions — code block, explanation, complexity, DSA & design patterns\n✅ 3 sets of 100% ATS-optimised resumes\n✅ 45-day learning calendar & roadmap\n✅ LinkedIn profile optimisation so you start getting interview calls\n✅ A fully functional explain-your-framework with end-to-end, real-time scenarios\n\nLike, follow, and comment anything and I’ll send you the link — at a flash-sale rate right now.\n▶️ Watching on YouTube? It’s in the pinned comment.\n\n— Sreenidhi Rajakrishnan | SDET Interview Prep\n\n#selenium #seleniumjava #testng #automationtesting #testautomation sdet qaengineer interviewprep softwaretesting flakytests qajobs testingtips",
