      {
        "id": "3972079774786638858",
        "__typename": "XDTGraphVideo",
        "shortcode": "DcfqYKbq9QK",
        "url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcfqYKbq9QK/",
        "caption": "This question was asked in a 35 LPA QA interview. Can you answer it? 💰\n\n\"How do you achieve parallel testing in Playwright?\"\n\nMost candidates say \"parallel workers\" and stop. Decent answer. But the moment you say SHARDING, the interviewer switches and asks the real question:\n\n\"What's the difference between parallel workers and sharding?\"\n\nHere's the answer that clears the round. 👇\n\n⚙️ Parallel workers = parallel tests on a SINGLE machine. You're limited by that machine's CPU cores.\n\n⚙️ Sharding = tests distributed across MULTIPLE machines. The suite gets split, executed in parallel, and results merge into one report. This is called scale-out.\n\nThe difference in numbers: 100 tests on one machine = ~5 minutes. Same suite with sharding = under 1 minute.\n\nThis is the depth that separates a 10 LPA answer from a 35 LPA answer.\n\nI've made detailed notes on Playwright sharding. Want them? Comment anything below and I'll send them right away. 📩\n\nparallel testing in Playwright, parallel workers vs sharding, Playwright interview question, 35 LPA QA interview, scale-out testing, Playwright sharding notes.\n\n#playwright #sdet #automationtesting  #techwithjatin #manualtesting",
